\section{Read the Argument Stack as Substitution}

In a Krivine machine for the pure lambda calculus, a closure is a term paired with an environment, and the environment maps variables to closures. Write a state as \(\langle t,\rho,\pi\rangle\), where \(\pi\) is a stack of suspended operands and its top is on the left. With named binders for readability, the transitions are
\[
\begin{aligned}
\langle t\,u,\rho,\pi\rangle &\longrightarrow
  \langle t,\rho,(u,\rho)::\pi\rangle,\\
\langle \lambda x.t,\rho,c::\pi\rangle &\longrightarrow
  \langle t,\rho[x\mapsto c],\pi\rangle,\\
\langle x,\rho,\pi\rangle &\longrightarrow
  \langle t',\rho',\pi\rangle
  &&\text{if \(\rho(x)=(t',\rho')\).}
\end{aligned}
\]
The last transition restores the argument's defining environment; keeping the callee's environment instead would implement the wrong binding discipline. The readback invariant for this machine interprets environments as simultaneous substitutions and the stack as pending applications. Application and lookup then reorganize the representation without changing its readback; entering a lambda with a pending operand accounts for a head beta step.

A lambda with an empty stack is an answer: the machine does not normalize its body. In \((\lambda x.\lambda y.y)\,\Omega\), the machine pushes a closure for \(\Omega\), binds \(x\), and stops at the inner lambda without entering \(\Omega\). If a body uses an argument twice, each lookup may restart evaluation of its defining expression. An environment makes substitution cheap, but does not by itself implement call-by-need.

\section{Make the Waiting Context Explicit}

A strict machine instead binds an already evaluated value. A simple left-to-right evaluator obtains a corresponding machine by replacing its recursive calls with two frame forms: ``evaluate this operand after the operator returns'' and ``apply this operator after the operand returns.'' A continuation stack holds these frames. For \((\lambda x.\lambda y.y)\,\Omega\), the first frame causes entry of \(\Omega\) even though the function ignores \(x\). A useful state invariant is that an apply frame contains an operator value and that an environment slot of the strict core contains a completed value, never a suspended operand. A return frame must retain the caller's environment and resume location; otherwise even a nonrecursive nested application can return into the wrong lexical context.

Xavier Leroy's \emph{From Krivine's machine to the Caml implementations} explains a different strict design, the ZINC Abstract Machine, or ZAM. Its application code evaluates operands right to left, then the operator; the callee takes the already computed values from an argument stack. A mark separates the current argument sequence from earlier stack contents, and \texttt{GRAB} consumes an argument or handles partial application. This resemblance to the Krivine machine lies in how functions consume pending arguments, not in when those arguments are evaluated. The order shown in Leroy's machine is a property of that design; a compiler for a language specifying left-to-right effects cannot adopt it unchanged.

\emph{The Modern Compiler Dossier}, Chapter~14, proposes generating execution engines from a shared semantic specification. For this example, each strategy first needs its own rule for application and return. Only then is it legitimate to compare, say, threaded dispatch and switch dispatch for the same machine. Treating call-by-name and call-by-value as dispatch variants would compare engines that need not even agree on termination.

\section{Worked Example: Fewer or More Arguments}

Suppose a strict push-enter machine compiles \(f=\lambda x.\lambda y.\lambda z.\,x+y+z\) with an entry that can consume three values before computing the sum. Calling \(f\) with just \(2\) must return a function retaining that argument; the partial-application entry reinstates \(2\) before consuming new arguments. Applying the result to \(3\) and \(4\) must yield \(9\), just as a saturated call does. In a saturated call the machine may enter the body without building the two intermediate function values. For an overapplication, use \(g=\lambda x.\,\mathit{id}\) with entry arity one and call \(g\,2\,3\). The body returns \(\mathit{id}\), to which the machine must apply the remaining \(3\) before resuming the caller. Returning \(\mathit{id}\) directly would silently discard the second operand.

Leroy contrasts push-enter with eval-apply: the latter checks or knows the closure's arity at the calling side and selects an appropriate application path. Test both protocols with unknown function values, not only direct calls whose arities were known at compile time. Include a closure returned by a function whose stack frame has already been reused, as in Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-171}. Such a closure needs persistent data even if the machine normally holds new argument bindings in stack slots. Separating tests for the argument protocol from tests for environment lifetime prevents a correct saturated call from hiding either a wrong resumption or an escaped stack reference.

