\section{Read Back a Suspended Argument Under the Right Binders}

An interpreter debugger can display the term represented by a Krivine state without taking a single machine step. The input is a state for the pure lambda calculus with an acyclic closure graph and globally resolved binder identifiers. The display routine unfolds captured terms in their original environments, wraps the control term in the pending applications, and prints distinct binders with distinct names. For a well-scoped reachable state its output is a closed term, modulo alpha-renaming, representing the current head-reduction problem. This is a diagnostic routine, not a second evaluator.

\begin{verbatim}
readback(term, env, stack):
  t = close(term, env)
  # The top argument is the first pending application.
  for (arg, saved) in stack from top to bottom:
    t = App(t, close(arg, saved))
  return print_with_distinct_names(t)
close(term, env):
  case term of
    Var(id):
      if id not in env: return term
      (arg, saved) = env[id]
      return close(arg, saved)
    Lam(id, body):
      # A bound occurrence stays under this lambda.
      return Lam(id, close(body, env without id))
    App(f, x): return App(close(f, env), close(x, env))
\end{verbatim}

Start with \((\lambda x.(\lambda y.y)\,x)\,(\lambda z.z)\). After the first lambda entry, the environment binds \(x\) to the identity closure. The displayed term is \((\lambda y.y)\,(\lambda z.z)\), which is also what the debugger prints after the next application transition, now with a pending stack entry for \(x\). After entering \(\lambda y.y\), the current control is \(y\) and its saved argument is \((x,\rho)\), where \(\rho\) binds \(x\). The display is \(\lambda z.z\), before lookup of \(y\) has executed. Forgetting the saved environment in readback can leave a misleading free \(x\) on the screen.

The check is that application and variable-lookup transitions preserve the displayed term up to alpha-renaming, whereas a lambda entry with a pending operand accounts for one head beta step. Since readback can duplicate a shared closure at every use, its textual output can grow exponentially in the size of the machine state. A practical debugger can cap expansion and show references to previously displayed closures, marking abbreviated results as such.
